1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,240
 Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:02,980
 So I hope you enjoyed that.

3
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:05,600
 Now, I want to make it real clear that when I give you

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:09,760
 a 15-minute video of an amazing inventor and computer

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,280
 scientist, you don't have to remember every word

6
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,160
 that that person says, OK?

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,160
 It's more important to get the gist of it.

8
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,520
 I try to cover the things I really

9
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,560
 want you to know in my slides.

10
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:25,200
 And you might want to listen to it more than once,

11
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,760
 or listen to my slides and then go back.

12
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,280
 But I don't want you to memorize it.

13
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:33,360
 I wanted to give you smart people.

14
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,880
 I want you to hear from the smart people who

15
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,800
 did all this cool work in their own words.

16
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,600
 But when we hear from their own words,

17
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,160
 they're sometimes pretty technical.

18
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:47,060
 So just relax and enjoy listening to these people

19
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,320
 and understand that you're not going to get everything.

20
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,020
 But hopefully, you'll come back and you'll

21
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:52,980
 get more and more later.

22
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:57,920
 So the idea was that he really started with this idea

23
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,680
 of wireless, which was we're going to share one medium.

24
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,640
 And the wireless and the wire, the ethernet wire

25
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:07,360
 that he built, the coaxial cable that he built,

26
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:11,720
 was like a giant radio, except it ran in the ceiling.

27
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,880
 And so the design ended up really simple.

28
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:17,160
 And that's been good for ethernet over the years.

29
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,680
 And later, Wi-Fi is a variation of ethernet.

30
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,840
 And so it turns out to be sometimes it's

31
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:26,720
 best to build something simple, but that just

32
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,720
 make it go really fast and make it really cheap.

33
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:32,160
 He also formed the company 3Com, which

34
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:35,720
 was one of the first manufacturers of PC cards.

35
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,740
 And so back in the day, you would go buy a PC

36
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:41,200
 and you would go buy a 3Com card and you'd plug it

37
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,840
 into the back of your computer.

38
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:47,360
 And so there was a time when San Francisco Stadium Candlestick

39
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,840
 Park was named 3Com Stadium.

40
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:54,800
 And our friend Bob Metcalfe was involved in all of that.

41
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:56,880
 So he's done many things throughout his life.

42
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,120
 And we're honored to have met him.

43
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:03,480
 So we started with this four-layer architecture

44
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,780
 that says we're going to break down a big problem of cooperating

45
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:09,480
 applications across various kinds of networks.

46
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:10,680
 We're going to break it down.

47
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:14,480
 And we just got done talking about the link layer.

48
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,920
 It's literally 20,000, maybe 50,000 engineers

49
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,360
 have spent the last 20 years figuring out

50
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:22,840
 how to make this work.

51
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:27,000
 Because the layered architecture lets them think,

52
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,560
 although it was informed, they have 20,000, 50,000--

53
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:36,240
 I don't know-- 20,000 engineers that think about that problem

54
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:39,000
 and they ignore the rest of the problems.

55
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,880
 They ignore the rest of the problems.

56
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:44,920
 That's great, because they've gotten really

57
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,960
 good at this one problem.

58
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,480
 Now we're going to go like-- I don't know how many engineers.

59
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:51,520
 Let's just make it up-- 5K engineers

60
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:53,480
 think about this next problem.

61
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:57,360
 The next problem is called-- oh, my color's got to change--

62
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,200
 the inter-network layer.

63
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:07,200
 It is the notion of forwarding each of the postcards

64
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,760
 with a from and a to address, forwarding enough times

65
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,240
 to get them all the way across the network.

66
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:15,680
 That's the next problem we're going to solve.

67
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,320
 We're going to stop thinking about the link layer.

68
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:19,860
 We're just going to assume it works.

69
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:20,800
 It's just magic.

70
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,960
 That's what a layered architecture gives you.

71
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:24,800
 You don't worry about the stuff above you.

72
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:26,640
 You don't worry about the stuff below you.

73
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:31,440
 You focus like crazy on the stuff that you're focusing on.

74
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:40,560
 So the link layer only works on one link.

75
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:42,440
 It worries about one link.

76
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,720
 And there might be 15 or so of these links.

77
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:49,160
 But the internet layer worries about all the links

78
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,920
 and the proper sequence of links to follow

79
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,720
 to get from UM to Stanford.

80
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,000
 That's a kind of complex problem.

81
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,920
 We're not even going to worry about reliability.

82
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:05,480
 We're just going to worry about if I had a postcard

83
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:09,480
 with a from and a to address, a packet, can I get it there?

84
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,240
 How will I forward it?

85
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:13,280
 I assume the link layer is perfect.

86
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:15,640
 So the internet layer is the first end to end.

87
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:18,760
 Because if you recall, this vertical set of boxes

88
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:21,360
 is the host starting computer.

89
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:25,000
 And this vertical set of boxes is the destination computer.

90
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,760
 So in this little Raspberry Pi, all four of these things--

91
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:34,080
 application, transport, IP, and the link--

92
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:37,800
 is all part of this one gadget.

93
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:39,360
 So IP is best effort.

94
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,480
 And it's OK to drop data if things go bad.

95
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,240
 And that's one of its most charming features.

96
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:48,520
 But what it had to introduce in addition to the ethernet

97
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:52,920
 addresses, or the media access layer, MAC addresses,

98
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:57,160
 is an address of the destination.

99
00:04:57,160 --> 00:04:59,280
 Now, if you remember, the MAC addresses,

100
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:00,800
 the addresses for the link layer,

101
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,080
 come from the manufacturer.

102
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,520
 The moment this equipment is manufactured,

103
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,000
 it is burned in a serial number.

104
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,040
 These move all around the world.

105
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,200
 If this runs here at the University of Michigan,

106
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:14,960
 it needs one address to connect to the network, one IP address.

107
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:17,880
 And if it runs in Stanford, it needs a different IP address.

108
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:19,420
 So we have to be able to change these.

109
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:21,440
 They're assigned differently.

110
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,880
 So it's the worldwide number.

111
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,560
 That is your postcard address.

112
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,280
 It's like a phone number, right?

113
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:32,840
 So wherever I'm at, you call my phone, and this gadget rings.

114
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:38,160
 So it's address-- well, that's actually kind of a bad example,

115
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:40,040
 because I use the same phone number.

116
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:41,560
 Ignore that.

117
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:42,600
 Phones use magic.

118
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:43,720
 They use crazy magic.

119
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:49,040
 So in computers, they're not as cool as phones.

120
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,040
 You have to change the address everywhere you go.

121
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,680
 We'll talk about how you change the address everywhere you go.

122
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:58,560
 The IP addresses are based on where the station is connected.

123
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:02,200
 They do get reorganized once in a great while, but not very often.

124
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:05,440
 And you can even go to various websites on the internet

125
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:10,360
 and say, IP address lookup is the most common search.

126
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:13,960
 And it will tell you something about where you're coming from, right?

127
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:15,660
 And it would kind of be sometimes really weird,

128
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,120
 because you might be at a Starbucks,

129
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:19,040
 and it might send it all to St. Louis, and it might say,

130
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:21,360
 oh, you're coming from St. Louis, even if you're not.

131
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:22,240
 So you can look it up.

132
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:24,200
 And you can look up other addresses as well.

133
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:26,280
 You can say, oh, here's an IP address.

134
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:27,600
 I'm going to look this one up.

135
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:29,520
 First, it starts by looking yours up.

136
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,760
 But you can put in different addresses,

137
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:33,520
 like in this particular one.

138
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:35,840
 If I put a different address in here,

139
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:40,080
 it will actually go look that address up.

140
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:43,120
 So the IP address format, it's four numbers

141
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:44,960
 that are separated by dots.

142
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:49,960
 Each of the numbers can be between 1 and 255.

143
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:53,200
 And it's just a representation of a 32-bit number.

144
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:55,640
 Back in the day, they kept the numbers small,

145
00:06:55,640 --> 00:07:00,280
 because they didn't want to use all the computer memory.

146
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:03,400
 And so this is an example of an IP address.

147
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:09,480
 Four numbers between 0 and 255, separated by dots.

148
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:14,920
 Now, the address is broken into two parts.

149
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:18,880
 There is the network number part, which is the prefix.

150
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:22,600
 And then there is the computer number within network.

151
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:24,440
 And it's kind of like phone numbers

152
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:29,720
 were before cell phones, where the area code was

153
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,160
 where the phone was.

154
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:34,800
 And then this was within that area code, where to find it.

155
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:38,840
 And even the older days, these were actually geographic too.

156
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,800
 So these numbers would be neighborhoods or whatever.

157
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:43,120
 These days, it's all electronic.

158
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:47,920
 So the precise mapping of a phone number to a geography

159
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:49,960
 is less precise.

160
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,780
 And that's why you get a cell phone number.

161
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,360
 And you move to a whole new state.

162
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:54,860
 You keep your old cell phone number,

163
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:56,560
 because it's become electronic.

164
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:59,760
 But in the early days, when it was actual relays and switches

165
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:02,400
 making the phone numbers work, they actually

166
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,000
 had to do with where.

167
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,040
 It's actually kind of fascinating how phones work.

168
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:11,960
 I ought to teach a class on how phone switches worked

169
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:13,480
 in the 1800s.

170
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:17,320
 Actually quite fascinating, because they

171
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,400
 were surprisingly simple.

172
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,000
 That's the interesting part.

173
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,600
 How simple turn of the century-- well,

174
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:24,880
 turn of last century-- phone numbers were.

175
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:28,760
 So let's get back to IP addresses.

176
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:30,480
 There's four numbers.

177
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:33,400
 Some part of those four numbers is a prefix.

178
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:36,000
 We call that the network number.

179
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,560
 And when the packet is in the middle of the network,

180
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:41,160
 it doesn't really look at these numbers.

181
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:42,400
 It only looks at the prefix.

182
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:44,840
 So it kind of thinks of all the packets going

183
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:49,920
 to a piece of the University of Michigan as 141, 211 star star.

184
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,940
 And then we assign these numbers within Michigan.

185
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,960
 And then this number is assigned to us

186
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:58,000
 by the internet authority that assigns numbers.

187
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:59,380
 So we say, hey, we need some more.

188
00:08:59,380 --> 00:09:00,600
 And they give us a prefix.

189
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:04,440
 Then within that prefix, we get to set up all the other things.

190
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:11,200
 So this network number, which is the prefix of the IP address,

191
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:14,620
 is the way that a packet is routed through the internet

192
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:17,080
 as it progresses through the internet.

193
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:23,600
 And so it's sort of like if I start with my computer here

194
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:27,120
 at U of M, and that's my number, that's my actual number.

195
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:31,000
 And here's a Stanford computer, and that's its number.

196
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,720
 And I send a packet.

197
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:38,640
 And I say, OK, I'm going to send it from 141, 211, 144, 188,

198
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:41,840
 to 67, 149, yada yada.

199
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:45,600
 As soon as the packet enters the network,

200
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:48,280
 it doesn't throw the data away, but it stops thinking about it.

201
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:50,280
 It only looks at the prefix.

202
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:55,440
 And so it greatly simplifies what the routers have to do

203
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:58,040
 as it goes across the hops in the internet.

204
00:09:58,040 --> 00:09:59,840
 It's greatly simplified because it only

205
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:00,960
 looks at the first part.

206
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:04,000
 It doesn't have to look at the whole thing.

207
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,460
 And then it starts making decisions

208
00:10:05,460 --> 00:10:06,400
 as to how to get there.

209
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,640
 It says, OK, I'll hop it over here.

210
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:10,000
 And it's like in Kansas City.

211
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,560
 And it says, what am I going to do here?

212
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,800
 And I'll pick one of those two links, and I'll go over here.

213
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:16,780
 And then as it exits the network,

214
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:19,700
 it dumps it somehow onto Stanford's campus.

215
00:10:19,700 --> 00:10:22,880
 And then it finds its way-- oh, it didn't find it to this one.

216
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:24,120
 It found its way to that one.

217
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:27,240
 There you go, unpredictable computers.

218
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:30,520
 So within the Stanford campus, these numbers mean something.

219
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:32,780
 And the Stanford campus uses those numbers

220
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:35,560
 to get the thing to the actual real computer.

221
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:38,480
 And so it sort of used this prefix.

222
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:40,640
 It has a real number, but then it

223
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,600
 uses the prefix in the middle.

224
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:45,480
 And then the real number reappears at the end.

225
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:47,320
 So that's called a network number.

226
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:51,200
 And it greatly simplifies.

227
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:55,440
 It's complex enough to be in the middle

228
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,640
 and have to worry about all these things coming from Kansas

229
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:02,080
 City and Beijing and where these things go.

230
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:04,660
 To have to look at each one and know where every computer is

231
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:06,040
 is crazy.

232
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:10,040
 So that's why they call it the inter-network protocol,

233
00:11:10,040 --> 00:11:12,160
 because it says there's one network,

234
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:14,920
 there's another network, and the only thing I care about

235
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:16,800
 is the network number.

236
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:19,280
 And I am the inter-network protocol,

237
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:22,460
 IP, inter-network protocol.

238
00:11:22,460 --> 00:11:24,280
 I'm just getting these things from one network

239
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:25,160
 to another network.

240
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:27,080
 And then it's up to that network to figure out

241
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:29,840
 how to get the darn thing to the right computer within it.

242
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,380
 And it's up to Stanford to figure out

243
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:34,120
 how to get it to the right computer at Stanford.

244
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:37,840
 So these things are called the network number.

245
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:39,960
 It's the prefix of the IP address.

246
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:42,200
 Now the key thing that-- it's really a beautiful, beautiful

247
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,440
 design, because the center of the network

248
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:49,560
 is both exceedingly complex in one way, that it's so big

249
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:52,200
 and so fast, but exceedingly simple in the other.

250
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:56,760
 It just has to move the data from point A to point B.

251
00:11:56,760 --> 00:11:59,400
 And if you think about it, it's even simpler.

252
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:03,160
 If we look at it from the perspective of this router,

253
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:06,240
 the router here I've got circled,

254
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:08,200
 it just received a packet.

255
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:10,000
 It looks at the network number.

256
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,360
 And it actually doesn't even care to some degree

257
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:14,240
 where it really belongs.

258
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:16,720
 It only has one of two choices.

259
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,120
 It's going to go this way.

260
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:20,880
 It's going to go that way.

261
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:22,680
 Which one's better?

262
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:27,400
 And it turns out, if you look at it, either one would work.

263
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:29,280
 Turns out this one is better.

264
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:32,240
 But if I went this way, it would just take an extra tenth

265
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,120
 of a second or something.

266
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:36,360
 So it turns out the decisions that are made

267
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:38,720
 don't even have to be perfect.

268
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:41,680
 You can make the wrong decision, and the network automatically

269
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:42,200
 corrects.

270
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,920
 That's part of the goal of the network.

271
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:47,720
 And so it really simplifies and limits

272
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:51,880
 the need for each router to understand

273
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:54,240
 the entire network, which makes these routers--

274
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:55,960
 I draw them small in these diagrams

275
00:12:55,960 --> 00:13:00,120
 on purpose, because we think of them as small and fast,

276
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:02,000
 and only solving a really tiny problem,

277
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,080
 but doing it really super awesome.

278
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:09,080
 So routers maintain what we call router tables.

279
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:12,360
 And they maintain a list of network numbers

280
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:17,800
 and the best outbound route for each of the network numbers.

281
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:19,480
 Now, the other thing that they do

282
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:22,560
 is they pass routes back and forth.

283
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:25,440
 And this is how they adapt to errors, right?

284
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:26,760
 They get updated dynamically.

285
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:29,060
 They ask each other for the best place.

286
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:31,480
 If they see a network number they haven't seen before,

287
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:34,600
 they ask their neighbors, then their neighbor's neighbors.

288
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:38,520
 And they go, oh, OK, I got a good way to get to 67, 149.

289
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:40,960
 And so there's all kinds of communication,

290
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:45,280
 but it's relatively slow and it doesn't have to be perfect.

291
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:47,560
 And so router tables are what routers have,

292
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:50,280
 but they're indexed by the network number of the packets,

293
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:52,480
 not the host of the destination.

294
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:55,960
 So that's an amazing, amazing improvement

295
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:59,000
 in the performance and efficiency of the internet

296
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,880
 core, the IP core.

297
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,600
 So it's really quite simple, right?

298
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:08,880
 You basically have a local area network on your campus.

299
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:11,360
 This might actually be your house, too.

300
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:13,840
 House is kind of like a campus.

301
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:16,000
 And you can do all the crazy things you want,

302
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,040
 hundreds of computers, hundreds of servers,

303
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:20,960
 and thousands of laptops.

304
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:27,360
 And you get one address that is the address of your campus.

305
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:29,640
 It's the network number of your campus

306
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:31,160
 to the rest of the world.

307
00:14:31,160 --> 00:14:35,200
 And then all over the world, people can send to you.

308
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:40,280
 And by simply looking at the prefix, data makes it to you.

309
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:45,760
 Keeps this really simple and really fast.

310
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:47,320
 So it's beautiful.

311
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:48,480
 That's with a network number.

312
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:50,800
 One area code, one network number.

313
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:52,560
 It's the only thing has to be kept track of.

314
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:56,800
 A whole UM campus can be characterized

315
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,900
 within the core of the network as basically a single network

316
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:00,400
 number.

317
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,560
 And the reality is, it's usually a few of these,

318
00:15:03,560 --> 00:15:06,160
 because they get given to you in smaller chunks.

319
00:15:06,160 --> 00:15:09,200
 So you end up with 20 or 30 of these things for your campus

320
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:11,080
 if you're a medium to large campus.

321
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:20,160
 So now I want to talk a little bit about the problem

322
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:23,320
 of computers that move around.

323
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:26,200
 So you have a laptop.

324
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:29,180
 And you use it at your local coffee shop.

325
00:15:29,180 --> 00:15:30,520
 And then you close the lid.

326
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:32,880
 And you go home and open it.

327
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:34,200
 And it works at home.

328
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:35,440
 And then you close the lid.

329
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:36,200
 And you go to school.

330
00:15:36,200 --> 00:15:38,280
 And you open it and talk to wireless at school.

331
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:39,520
 And it works.

332
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:41,600
 It's like, hey, Dr. Chuck, you just

333
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:43,580
 told me that I have to have this address.

334
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:45,120
 And I can't talk on the internet if I

335
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:47,000
 don't have the right address.

336
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:50,160
 And the packets are routed based on the prefix of this address.

337
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:52,920
 Why is it that I can be three places--

338
00:15:52,920 --> 00:15:56,120
 coffee shop, home, and school--

339
00:15:56,120 --> 00:15:57,680
 and it seems like everything works?

340
00:15:57,680 --> 00:16:02,960
 Well, that's because while your computer has an ethernet

341
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:05,800
 address that's baked in at the factory,

342
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:08,880
 most computers are configured when they first open up

343
00:16:08,880 --> 00:16:11,680
 and connect to a Wi-Fi to ask, instead

344
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:14,560
 of having an IP address configured in your computer,

345
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:19,600
 they send a request out to say, hey, I'm new here.

346
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:22,120
 Is there anyone who will give me an IP address that I might

347
00:16:22,120 --> 00:16:25,240
 use for this particular location?

348
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:29,480
 And if there is an access point, say, at your Starbucks,

349
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:33,560
 it says, sure, use this one.

350
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:34,840
 You're at Starbucks.

351
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:36,880
 That's a good number for Starbucks.

352
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:39,120
 Now, it turns out that the prefix of this

353
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:41,840
 is exactly the prefix that the world sees

354
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,640
 all this traffic to Starbucks.

355
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:46,800
 So it's actually kind of a real address.

356
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:50,280
 And it gives you an address.

357
00:16:50,280 --> 00:16:53,560
 So you ask-- when your thing comes up, it asks,

358
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:54,800
 what can I get?

359
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:56,880
 And then it's told what address to use.

360
00:16:56,880 --> 00:16:59,400
 So you're using one address while you're at Starbucks,

361
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:02,280
 a different address at home, and a different address yet again

362
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:04,200
 at school.

363
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:10,240
 So that's called the dynamic host configuration protocol.

364
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:14,800
 Now, it turns out with schools and homes

365
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:20,400
 and there's just too many computers

366
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,280
 to give every computer a real address.

367
00:17:24,280 --> 00:17:27,240
 And so we have these special addresses.

368
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:30,480
 They're called the non-routable addresses.

369
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:33,600
 So you'll probably notice if you go to one person's house,

370
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:38,920
 you will have an IP address of 192.168.something.something.

371
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:40,280
 Go to a different person's house,

372
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:43,920
 it'll also be 192.168.something.something.

373
00:17:43,920 --> 00:17:48,000
 And you go to your house, it's 192.168.something.something.

374
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:49,800
 And you're like, how can that work?

375
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:51,520
 I seem to have the same address.

376
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:54,920
 Well, that's because of a technology called network

377
00:17:54,920 --> 00:18:00,160
 address translation, where each of the home routers

378
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:02,480
 actually has a unique and distinct address,

379
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:04,160
 but you're not seeing it.

380
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:05,960
 It's giving you a temporary address,

381
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:09,000
 an address that really can't run at all on the internet.

382
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,120
 It only lives within the house.

383
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:14,720
 But then as your packets leave the house or leave the Starbucks,

384
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:16,000
 the real address is put in.

385
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,480
 And then when it comes back, the real address is taken out

386
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:20,440
 and your local address is put in.

387
00:18:20,440 --> 00:18:22,280
 So they're called non-routable addresses,

388
00:18:22,280 --> 00:18:24,780
 because if they ever escaped into the real internet,

389
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:27,560
 they'd be like, oh, those are for your house.

390
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:29,120
 They're not supposed to go very far.

391
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:30,680
 And I don't even know where they go.

392
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:33,400
 They go nowhere in the core of the internet,

393
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:36,240
 but they go properly inside your house.

394
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:39,000
 So the way this ends up working is

395
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:43,080
 if you are at your coffee shop, the coffee shop

396
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:49,720
 has an address you associate with their base access point.

397
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:53,360
 It gives you a non-routable address to use locally.

398
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:56,400
 And then as your traffic gets sent,

399
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:59,480
 this address is changed to that address.

400
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:01,000
 You never see this happening.

401
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:04,440
 It's done as the packet goes through the base station.

402
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:06,240
 And then when the packet comes back,

403
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:08,200
 it comes back with this address.

404
00:19:08,200 --> 00:19:09,920
 But then as it goes to the base station,

405
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:12,240
 it switches back to this address.

406
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:14,760
 So even though this address is not the real address

407
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:18,800
 the network sees, they see you having this address here.

408
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:22,000
 So if you would do an IP lookup from a coffee shop,

409
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:23,600
 the coffee shop will be identified.

410
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:25,240
 You will see the IP address here.

411
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:27,160
 And if you looked at your laptop,

412
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:28,960
 your laptop would not have that same address

413
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:37,480
 because it's being translated in the access point.

414
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:43,280
 So then you go home.

415
00:19:46,040 --> 00:19:52,980
 And at home, your access point has a different address.

416
00:19:52,980 --> 00:20:01,640
 But your computer asks your access point for an address.

417
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:04,120
 And it gets a local address that's generated locally.

418
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:06,040
 And again, there's a mapping.

419
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:13,800
 It's called NAT, N-A-T, network address translation.

420
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:17,600
 So as the packet goes through, it takes out the one address

421
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:18,880
 and puts in the second.

422
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:19,600
 Data comes back.

423
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:22,440
 It takes the address out and puts the local one in.

424
00:20:22,440 --> 00:20:24,360
 And the same thing happens at school.

425
00:20:24,360 --> 00:20:25,920
 So you're at school finally.

426
00:20:25,920 --> 00:20:27,400
 And you get a different address.

427
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:28,880
 They're not the same, but you look,

428
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:30,200
 and the prefixes are the same.

429
00:20:30,200 --> 00:20:31,920
 It's like they should be the same place.

430
00:20:31,920 --> 00:20:34,080
 But they're completely different because your school

431
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:35,520
 has a different address.

432
00:20:35,520 --> 00:20:38,200
 And there is a translation that goes back and forth

433
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:40,000
 as the data goes around and around.

434
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:43,360
 So just for the distance of the local Wi-Fi

435
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:47,160
 or whatever, use these 192.168 numbers.

436
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:49,760
 And then they're translated to the real numbers

437
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:52,080
 by your network access points.

438
00:20:52,080 --> 00:20:55,840
 So these are illegal inside this network.

439
00:20:55,840 --> 00:21:00,340
 So if it ever saw a 192.168, it would just

440
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:03,400
 throw that packet away.

441
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:08,400
 They're only for very local connections.

442
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:14,120
 So with that, I'm wondering if by now you

443
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:17,320
 know why this is funny.

444
00:21:17,320 --> 00:21:22,840
 I'll give you a minute.

445
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:36,560
 OK, so the reason this is funny is she

446
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:40,520
 traced the killer's IP address, and it

447
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:44,960
 had a prefix of 192.168, which means

448
00:21:44,960 --> 00:21:49,600
 the killer had to be within a relatively short radius,

449
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:53,800
 using likely the same Wi-Fi access point, which

450
00:21:53,800 --> 00:21:57,120
 means it was close.

451
00:21:57,120 --> 00:21:59,960
 Very scary.

452
00:21:59,960 --> 00:22:02,480
 Hope you think it's funny.

453
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:06,000
 Of course, XKCD is never exactly funny,

454
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
 but it hopefully makes you smile a little bit.

455
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:24,520
 So up to now, I've been talking about this cloud network that's

456
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:27,200
 magic, and the packets take different routes.

457
00:22:27,200 --> 00:22:29,440
 And we don't know, but they show up.

458
00:22:29,440 --> 00:22:30,920
 Remember the mailbox?

459
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,320
 They just show up or don't show up, right?

460
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:38,640
 So it turns out that sometimes we, as engineers,

461
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:41,880
 want to take a look at what's going on inside the internet.

462
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:45,920
 And it turned out that there was a feature they added early

463
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:48,760
 on to help diagnose problems in the internet

464
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:50,480
 that we still use today.

465
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:52,840
 And it's so convenient that it's built right

466
00:22:52,840 --> 00:22:54,520
 into your operating system.

467
00:22:54,520 --> 00:22:57,800
 If you have a Macintosh or Linux, Traceroute is built in.

468
00:22:57,800 --> 00:23:00,260
 And if you have Windows, you've got to install Traceroute.

469
00:23:00,260 --> 00:23:03,920
 So just say Windows, install Traceroute, and you'll find something.

470
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:06,480
 But there was a problem.

471
00:23:06,480 --> 00:23:13,440
 So if you recall, I said that each router sees the world very narrowly

472
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:18,040
 and simply sees a packet and makes a decision one place.

473
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:23,680
 So let's say that here comes a packet on the way to Stanford.

474
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:27,040
 And these routers are sort of strangely configured.

475
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:29,300
 This router thinks it should go there.

476
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:31,600
 This router thinks it should go there.

477
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:33,840
 And this router thinks it should go there.

478
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:34,600
 Well, it comes in.

479
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:37,600
 It goes like, oh, well, I know where that one goes, there.

480
00:23:37,600 --> 00:23:42,920
 So if you end up with this misconfigured router situation,

481
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:48,320
 you end up with your data going round and round in circles.

482
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:53,320
 It would actually crush the network, because it's like a whirlpool.

483
00:23:53,320 --> 00:23:55,260
 You can't even notice that it's happening,

484
00:23:55,260 --> 00:24:00,160
 because there might actually be 10.

485
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:01,620
 There might be a bunch of them.

486
00:24:01,620 --> 00:24:04,420
 And then it comes back, and you send it around again.

487
00:24:04,420 --> 00:24:06,000
 So you're filling up all your bandwidth.

488
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,320
 It's never going to get there unless something changes,

489
00:24:09,320 --> 00:24:11,080
 unless something crashes.

490
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:13,380
 It's not going to change, because these routers think

491
00:24:13,380 --> 00:24:14,580
 that's the best thing to do.

492
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:15,680
 They're mistaken.

493
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:16,380
 But they are.

494
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:18,080
 They can be mistaken, because they're

495
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:22,160
 operating with imperfect information.

496
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:26,240
 So how would you solve this problem?

497
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:28,440
 Routers are imperfect.

498
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:30,000
 So they solve the problem with a thing

499
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,560
 called the time to live field.

500
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:35,240
 So much like the network address translation

501
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:38,200
 tweaks the addresses on the way in and on the way out,

502
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:41,440
 the time to live is a field that routers change.

503
00:24:41,440 --> 00:24:43,540
 Every time a packet goes through a router,

504
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:46,200
 it subtracts one from this field.

505
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:49,160
 And it starts with a number between 25.

506
00:24:49,160 --> 00:24:52,320
 It can be as high as 255, but it's usually like 25.

507
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:56,360
 And what happens is every time the packet

508
00:24:56,360 --> 00:25:00,080
 goes through a router, the number goes down by one.

509
00:25:00,080 --> 00:25:03,960
 So if it was-- it would come in here as 255.

510
00:25:03,960 --> 00:25:07,600
 Then it would go through here at 254, and 253.

511
00:25:07,600 --> 00:25:08,600
 It would come back.

512
00:25:08,600 --> 00:25:10,200
 It would be 252.

513
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:12,400
 And what would happen is eventually it

514
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:16,100
 would get to one of these guys, and it hit 0.

515
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:19,880
 And they would decide, OK, you've been running around

516
00:25:19,880 --> 00:25:20,800
 too long.

517
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:22,720
 We will throw you away.

518
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:26,680
 So the number goes down, and it always goes down.

519
00:25:26,680 --> 00:25:29,640
 But then when it hits 0, they throw the packet away.

520
00:25:29,640 --> 00:25:33,560
 They say, you have been through 255 hops.

521
00:25:33,560 --> 00:25:35,120
 Chances are good you're never going

522
00:25:35,120 --> 00:25:37,320
 to get to your destination.

523
00:25:37,320 --> 00:25:41,880
 So the traceroute command sort of cheats.

524
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:46,760
 Normal packets are sent with a TTL, or time to live, of like 30.

525
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:49,120
 30 hops, or 40 hops.

526
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:54,400
 But what traceroute does is sends broken packets.

527
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:58,560
 It turns out that when a router throws away your packet,

528
00:25:58,560 --> 00:26:00,640
 most of the time it is courteous.

529
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:03,880
 And it sends you back a notification and says, hi.

530
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:05,040
 I got your packet.

531
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:09,800
 I decremented it, subtracted one, and it got to 0.

532
00:26:09,800 --> 00:26:12,080
 And I threw it away.

533
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:13,520
 Sorry about that.

534
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:14,960
 Here's who I am.

535
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:17,320
 I mean, I really feel bad about throwing your packet away.

536
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:18,680
 Maybe you want to figure something else out.

537
00:26:18,680 --> 00:26:19,000
 I don't know.

538
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:20,360
 Something must be messed up.

539
00:26:20,360 --> 00:26:22,800
 It can't be my fault, but I threw it away.

540
00:26:22,800 --> 00:26:24,960
 So what traceroute does is it first sends a packet

541
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:26,840
 with a time to live of 1.

542
00:26:26,840 --> 00:26:28,600
 So the first router goes like, whoa, this

543
00:26:28,600 --> 00:26:30,440
 has been around a long time.

544
00:26:30,440 --> 00:26:31,840
 Sets it to 0, throws it away.

545
00:26:31,840 --> 00:26:33,440
 Then sends a note back.

546
00:26:33,440 --> 00:26:36,480
 Then traceroute sends a packet of 2 across.

547
00:26:36,480 --> 00:26:38,680
 It goes hop, hop, and it gets thrown away,

548
00:26:38,680 --> 00:26:40,440
 and a little note comes back.

549
00:26:40,440 --> 00:26:44,240
 So you can kind of build a map by sending enough packets

550
00:26:44,240 --> 00:26:47,400
 and getting kind of a return rejection

551
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:49,760
 from one of the routers.

552
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:51,280
 It would have got there.

553
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:53,880
 So if, for example, I do a traceroute

554
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:58,560
 from the University of Michigan to Stanford, if I did it now,

555
00:26:58,560 --> 00:27:00,960
 it'd be a different set of things.

556
00:27:00,960 --> 00:27:03,200
 I get this output.

557
00:27:03,200 --> 00:27:05,640
 And if I take a look at this, this is the hop.

558
00:27:05,640 --> 00:27:09,320
 So there's the first 1, 2, 3, 4, through 14.

559
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:10,520
 14 hops.

560
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:11,600
 So it takes 14 hops.

561
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:13,160
 Now interestingly, again, I don't

562
00:27:13,160 --> 00:27:15,960
 remember what the hop count was in the store and forward days,

563
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:17,520
 but it's quite a few.

564
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:21,960
 And that's because it's optimizing geography.

565
00:27:21,960 --> 00:27:27,120
 And so you can see the first hop is on my campus.

566
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:31,120
 Then the second and third hop are bouncing around my campus

567
00:27:31,120 --> 00:27:32,480
 some more.

568
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:34,640
 Now we don't quite know where this one is,

569
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:37,600
 but now it's on a national network called Internet 2,

570
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:39,320
 Internet 2, Internet 2.

571
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:42,440
 This is probably going across the country.

572
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:45,800
 And then it ends up on Scenic, which I think

573
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:47,920
 is sort of California's network.

574
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:50,520
 It bounces through California a couple times.

575
00:27:50,520 --> 00:27:52,520
 Let's see.

576
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:56,240
 HPRLAX, that's Los Angeles.

577
00:27:56,240 --> 00:27:58,320
 This is-- I don't know where that is.

578
00:27:58,320 --> 00:27:59,880
 Los Angeles.

579
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:02,520
 Now it looks like it's making it to Oakland.

580
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:03,140
 I don't know.

581
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:05,060
 There's probably some meaning to these things.

582
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:07,360
 So it's making it through Oakland.

583
00:28:07,360 --> 00:28:11,280
 And then it's going to Stanford from something Oakland.

584
00:28:11,280 --> 00:28:12,960
 I don't know.

585
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:14,840
 But now it's on the Stanford campus.

586
00:28:14,840 --> 00:28:16,440
 And now it has three more hops to get

587
00:28:16,440 --> 00:28:20,400
 across the Stanford campus to the Stanford campus' web server.

588
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:24,080
 Now what's also going on here is it's keeping track

589
00:28:24,080 --> 00:28:25,840
 of how long it's taking.

590
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:28,120
 It sends each one a couple of times.

591
00:28:28,120 --> 00:28:30,080
 And so these are milliseconds.

592
00:28:30,080 --> 00:28:32,920
 So milliseconds are thousandths of a second.

593
00:28:32,920 --> 00:28:36,960
 So 534 milliseconds is a half a second.

594
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:38,880
 490 is a half a second.

595
00:28:38,880 --> 00:28:42,800
 So these are like half a second, half a second.

596
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:43,840
 Oh, wait, no, no.

597
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:45,400
 Sorry, not half a second.

598
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:46,800
 That'd be 4,000.

599
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:49,840
 Well, 1,000 milliseconds would be one second.

600
00:28:49,840 --> 00:28:51,360
 So I got that all wrong.

601
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:56,680
 So 0.49 milliseconds is like half of a thousandth of a second.

602
00:28:56,680 --> 00:28:59,040
 So that's fast, fast.

603
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:03,400
 Six milliseconds is one thousandth of a second.

604
00:29:03,400 --> 00:29:08,520
 76 milliseconds, which is nine hops away.

605
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:12,880
 That's 76 thousandths, or 7/100.

606
00:29:12,880 --> 00:29:18,760
 So it takes about 7/100.

607
00:29:18,760 --> 00:29:23,480
 So like 77 over 1,000.

608
00:29:23,480 --> 00:29:29,640
 So no, 7/100, sorry, 7/100 of a second.

609
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:34,840
 So less than a tenth of a second to get through 14 routers

610
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:37,520
 from Michigan to Stanford.

611
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:40,120
 Less than a tenth of a second.

612
00:29:40,120 --> 00:29:42,400
 Pretty impressive.

613
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:47,280
 Now, if I do a trace route from Ann Arbor, Michigan,

614
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:50,440
 to East Lansing, Michigan, Michigan State University,

615
00:29:50,440 --> 00:29:52,080
 we have a very close connection.

616
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:54,360
 I mentioned the Merit Network, where we've had a close

617
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:55,600
 connection for a long time.

618
00:29:55,600 --> 00:30:04,520
 So not only is it fewer hops, it's only eight hops

619
00:30:04,520 --> 00:30:06,760
 to get to Michigan State.

620
00:30:06,760 --> 00:30:10,280
 If you look at the hops here, I'm

621
00:30:10,280 --> 00:30:14,320
 bouncing around the campus for three.

622
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:17,120
 I'm bouncing through the state for two,

623
00:30:17,120 --> 00:30:21,160
 and bouncing on the Michigan State campus for three.

624
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:25,760
 So it's a total of eight, three hops to get across my campus,

625
00:30:25,760 --> 00:30:28,240
 two hops to get across the state of Michigan,

626
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:31,080
 and three hops to get on campus.

627
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:33,440
 And it's really fast.

628
00:30:33,440 --> 00:30:39,880
 It's 9/1000 of a second, which is less than 1/100 of a second.

629
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:41,240
 So really fast.

630
00:30:41,240 --> 00:30:42,360
 You can kind of see it.

631
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:44,920
 Now, if you ran this trace route more than once,

632
00:30:44,920 --> 00:30:46,200
 this might change.

633
00:30:46,200 --> 00:30:50,160
 It doesn't change too fast, but legally it could change.

634
00:30:50,160 --> 00:30:52,880
 I mean, there's no guarantee it's going to be the same.

635
00:30:52,880 --> 00:30:55,520
 It's highly likely it's going to be the same, because the most

636
00:30:55,520 --> 00:30:58,640
 efficient way is not going to change within a few seconds.

637
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:00,320
 But if you start in the middle of the day,

638
00:31:00,320 --> 00:31:02,840
 and you do it the next day, it might change quite a bit.

639
00:31:02,840 --> 00:31:05,800
 So it'd be something to play with.

640
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:09,880
 Run it, print it out, run it again at midnight,

641
00:31:09,880 --> 00:31:14,080
 run it in at noon, see if your trace route is different.

642
00:31:14,080 --> 00:31:16,320
 Interesting.

643
00:31:16,320 --> 00:31:18,760
 So here's an example of a trace route

644
00:31:18,760 --> 00:31:24,640
 from University of Michigan to Peking University in China.

645
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:31,280
 And so again, it bounces through my campus

646
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:33,800
 for a couple of hops.

647
00:31:33,800 --> 00:31:35,680
 My campus for a couple of hops.

648
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:40,720
 It bounces around the United States for a couple of hops.

649
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:45,400
 And then it starts crossing the Pacific Ocean.

650
00:31:45,400 --> 00:31:48,920
 There's traffic actually went through Seoul, Korea.

651
00:31:48,920 --> 00:31:50,800
 And then it ended up in Beijing.

652
00:31:50,800 --> 00:31:53,640
 Now, the interesting thing is you can see that it looks like

653
00:31:53,640 --> 00:31:59,760
 it's taking about 61,000 or 6/100, just over a--

654
00:31:59,760 --> 00:32:02,880
 just almost, well, half a tenth of a second,

655
00:32:02,880 --> 00:32:05,240
 to get across the country.

656
00:32:05,240 --> 00:32:07,040
 And then it starts taking longer.

657
00:32:07,040 --> 00:32:11,120
 And then by the time it's going all the way to China and back,

658
00:32:11,120 --> 00:32:13,480
 it's doing 256 milliseconds, which

659
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:15,840
 is about a quarter of a second.

660
00:32:15,840 --> 00:32:20,600
 Now, the big difference here is likely not traffic.

661
00:32:20,600 --> 00:32:23,840
 It is likely the speed of light.

662
00:32:23,840 --> 00:32:30,040
 It is how fast it takes light to get across the Pacific Ocean.

663
00:32:30,040 --> 00:32:32,600
 So it takes a while.

664
00:32:32,600 --> 00:32:37,040
 The reason I think it's most likely that these numbers are

665
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:46,920
 pretty consistent [INAUDIBLE]

666
00:32:46,920 --> 00:32:49,920
 It's low traffic because these are very consistent.

667
00:32:49,920 --> 00:32:54,000
 [INAUDIBLE]

668
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:55,360
 Pretty much all the time.

669
00:32:55,360 --> 00:32:57,800
 So that suggests that we're not waiting for any traffic.

670
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:00,520
 We're getting through as fast as we physically can,

671
00:33:00,520 --> 00:33:02,880
 which is some combination of the speed of the link

672
00:33:02,880 --> 00:33:03,880
 and the speed of light.

673
00:33:07,540 --> 00:33:10,440
 [KNOCKING]

674
00:33:10,440 --> 00:33:17,000
 So we just got done talking about how we add to every packet

675
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:20,680
 this global number, the IP address.

676
00:33:20,680 --> 00:33:23,120
 It has the prefix of the network number.

677
00:33:23,120 --> 00:33:25,680
 In the internet part, the internetwork,

678
00:33:25,680 --> 00:33:28,160
 where it's really moving data from one network

679
00:33:28,160 --> 00:33:31,120
 to another network and leaving it up to those destination

680
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:36,560
 networks, how to move it, we end up really simplifying

681
00:33:36,560 --> 00:33:37,360
 the postcard.

682
00:33:37,360 --> 00:33:41,400
 And the postcard ends up being a really apt example.

683
00:33:41,400 --> 00:33:43,520
 But the key thing, especially when

684
00:33:43,520 --> 00:33:45,480
 thinking about the four layer architecture,

685
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:48,320
 the reason that I think TCP/IP succeeded

686
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:50,680
 was in this real complex problem of moving data

687
00:33:50,680 --> 00:33:52,680
 between billions of computers.

688
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:55,880
 It kept the part in the middle real simple.

689
00:33:55,880 --> 00:33:57,240
 It doesn't try to be perfect.

690
00:33:57,240 --> 00:33:59,320
 It doesn't try to retransmit data.

691
00:33:59,320 --> 00:34:00,480
 It doesn't try to store it.

692
00:34:00,480 --> 00:34:02,400
 It doesn't try to keep it in the right order.

693
00:34:02,400 --> 00:34:04,520
 It doesn't try to say that if this packet went here,

694
00:34:04,520 --> 00:34:06,520
 I'm going to make sure the next one goes there.

695
00:34:06,520 --> 00:34:10,960
 The two packets, if one gets ahead, we don't really care.

696
00:34:10,960 --> 00:34:14,440
 It means it really fast and really scalable.

697
00:34:14,440 --> 00:34:19,960
 And by keeping it simple and really fast,

698
00:34:19,960 --> 00:34:21,800
 it solves really an amazing problem.

699
00:34:21,800 --> 00:34:24,320
 But we yet have other problems to solve.

700
00:34:24,320 --> 00:34:29,920
 So I want to close this lecture by introducing you

701
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:31,640
 to another person.

702
00:34:31,640 --> 00:34:34,560
 So Vint Cerf was a graduate student,

703
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:38,680
 as the whole notion of packet switching was being examined,

704
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:42,440
 both in the federal government at the Defense Advanced

705
00:34:42,440 --> 00:34:47,760
 Research Project Agency, DARPA, and in higher education,

706
00:34:47,760 --> 00:34:52,480
 both in the United States and Europe, UK, and elsewhere.

707
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:56,160
 And so Vint Cerf was at the right place at the right time.

708
00:34:56,160 --> 00:34:58,480
 And we can be very thankful for that.

709
00:34:58,480 --> 00:35:02,440
 He's credited as being one of the fathers of the ARPANET,

710
00:35:02,440 --> 00:35:06,400
 which, of course, begat the internet.

711
00:35:06,400 --> 00:35:10,400
 And so Vint is going to talk to us, in a sense,

712
00:35:10,400 --> 00:35:15,880
 going back even pre-ARPANET, and bring us up packets

713
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:18,880
 and what packets mean, and then how that all flowed

714
00:35:18,880 --> 00:35:23,120
 into the ARPANET, and how the ARPANET evolved

715
00:35:23,120 --> 00:35:24,960
 to become the internet.

716
00:35:24,960 --> 00:35:26,520
 Enjoy.

